From: Devin <devin@cognition.ai>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 14/14] stamp-p4: don't let a client channel-eof hangup a
 fresh pty session child

Issue #53.  Measured failure on m3-lab (OpenSSH client on the LAN):

    ssh -tt m5@10.255.10.160 'echo X' </dev/null
      -> exit-signal, no output, and
         "binfmt_flat: Unable to read code+data+bss, errno -4" in dmesg

while the identical exec session succeeds over loopback
(dbclient -t m5@127.0.0.1 'cmd') and succeeds over the LAN as soon
as stdin is held open:

    (sleep 3) | ssh -tt m5@10.255.10.160 'echo X'   -> works

When the client's stdin is already at EOF, OpenSSH sends
SSH_MSG_CHANNEL_EOF immediately after the exec request.  That lands
in recv_msg_channel_eof() -> check_close() ->
close_chan_fd(channel->writefd, SHUT_WR).  ptycommand() wires the pty
master as both readfd and writefd and sets bidir_fd = 0, so
close_chan_fd() takes the non-socket branch and m_close()s the
master outright.  That is the last master fd: the pty driver's
vhangup sends SIGHUP to the freshly created session leader, and the
signal lands while the vfork child is still inside execve() - the
bFLT load is slow enough on this target that the eof always arrives
first -> flat loader read returns -EINTR -> exec fails ->
binfmt_flat errno -4 -> exit-signal to the client.

The same race exists upstream; on a fast machine the child usually
execs and produces output before the eof is processed, so it goes
unnoticed.  The NOMMU flat loader stretches the window enough that
the child loses every time.

Set bidir_fd = 1 on the session channel in ptycommand():
close_chan_fd() then takes the socket path - shutdown(master,
SHUT_WR) fails harmlessly with ENOTSOCK, writefd is marked
FD_CLOSED (input forwarding stops, as it should), and the master
stays open so the child keeps running and its output keeps flowing.
The single m_close(master) still happens at channel teardown once
readfd/writefd/errfd are all FD_CLOSED.  A pty cannot convey EOF to
the child's stdin anyway, so this also matches OpenSSH server
behaviour: the remote command runs until it exits.

noptycommand() keeps bidir_fd = 0: writefd there is a real pipe and
hard-closing it is what delivers stdin EOF to the child.
---
 src/svr-chansession.c | 10 +++++++++-
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/src/svr-chansession.c
+++ b/src/svr-chansession.c
@@ -1028,7 +1028,15 @@
 		channel->readfd = chansess->master;
 		/* don't need to set stderr here */
 		ses.maxfd = MAX(ses.maxfd, chansess->master);
-		channel->bidir_fd = 0;
+		/* Keep bidir_fd set so a client channel-eof takes the
+		 * socket path in close_chan_fd(): shutdown() on a pty
+		 * fails harmlessly (ENOTSOCK), writefd is marked
+		 * FD_CLOSED, and the master stays open.  With bidir_fd
+		 * cleared the eof would m_close() the last master fd,
+		 * vhangup would SIGHUP the fresh session leader, and on
+		 * this slow NOMMU target the signal lands inside the
+		 * child's execve() (binfmt_flat -EINTR). */
+		channel->bidir_fd = 1;
 
 		setnonblocking(chansess->master);
 
